WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.624
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.624 --> 00:00:07.950
JOHN BARRUS: And welcome to
the supercharge performance

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:08.880
session.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:10.620
We're going to talk
about GPUs today.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:16.434
How many of you have used
GPUs in a data center before?

00:00:16.434 --> 00:00:17.100
Raise your hand.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:17.766
Oh, quite a few.

00:00:17.766 --> 00:00:18.480
That's good.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:19.690
How many in the cloud--

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:21.160
used them in the cloud before?

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:21.659
OK.

00:00:21.659 --> 00:00:23.734
So some of you had
the fun experience

00:00:23.734 --> 00:00:24.900
of setting up your own GPUs.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:27.900
And some have had the experience
of spinning them up and just

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:29.950
using them remotely.

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:32.820
So we're going to talk about why
people are interested in GPUs;

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:35.640
and we're going to tell
you about how GPUs are

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.980
available in Google
Compute Engine;

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:41.490
and remind you why you should be
so happy about it-- and anyone

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:44.274
that's set up GPUs before
should know that already.

00:00:44.274 --> 00:00:45.690
And then I'll walk
you through how

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:47.280
you provision a GPU instance.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.380
But the highlight of our
talk today will be three

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:54.180
demonstrations-- one from MapD,
who will be showing amazing

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:55.980
data analytics--

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:58.687
very fast data analytics
using GPUs in the cloud.

00:00:58.687 --> 00:01:00.270
And then Schlumberger
is going to talk

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:03.990
about how they do seismic high
performance computing with GPUs

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:05.280
in Google Cloud.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.414
And then we'll talk about
remote workstations.

00:01:08.414 --> 00:01:09.580
So why don't we get started?

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:12.060
So this must be
familiar to most of you,

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:14.940
if you've done much scientific
or engineering work, or work

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:16.410
in machine learning,
you know a lot

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:18.570
of that requires
matrix operations.

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:22.800
And these matrices can be very
large, sometimes 1,000 by 1,000

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:23.839
or larger.

00:01:23.839 --> 00:01:25.380
And the nice thing
about GPUs is they

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:27.240
can do some of these
operations in parallel.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:31.110
So, for instance, multiplying
ax in order to get b--

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:33.090
each individual element
of a can be multiplied

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:35.490
by an element of x in
parallel, independently

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:36.561
of all the other ones.

00:01:36.561 --> 00:01:38.310
But, of course, then
you have to gather up

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:41.737
some of that information
not quite as parallel

00:01:41.737 --> 00:01:44.070
when it comes time to add
those together, but still very

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:45.690
parallel.

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:48.060
And the nice thing
about GPUs is they

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:50.670
have a lot of cores for doing
these operations in parallel.

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:53.100
So just a quick overview--

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:56.790
CPUs, typically,
have on the order

00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:58.860
of dozens of threads that
can do these operations

00:01:58.860 --> 00:01:59.580
simultaneously.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:03.990
But GPUs have, in some cases,
two orders of magnitude--

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:05.500
100 times as many.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:09.152
And so they can do these
calculations simultaneously.

00:02:09.152 --> 00:02:10.860
And the other thing
that's nice with GPUs

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:12.970
is they have lots of
memory bandwidth--

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:16.060
sometimes an order of magnitude
additional memory bandwidth.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:18.390
So if you have a lot of
data to churn through,

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:22.320
you can push data through very
quickly through those cores.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:25.200
So I have a little animation
to show you how that works.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.570
A CPU is very
flexible, can do lots

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:30.600
of different types of operations
on the data that flows through.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:34.230
And it can scale up to dozens
of cores simultaneously.

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:36.110
So you can crank a lot
of data through a CPU.

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:39.090
A GPU has higher
memory bandwidth

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:40.740
so it can push
some data through--

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:42.690
streaming data
through-- more quickly.

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:45.630
And then can scale up
to thousands of cores

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:46.300
simultaneously.

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:48.860
So you can imagine, if you
have lots of operations to do,

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:52.250
you can do a lot more
work all at once.

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:57.290
So one example of that is AMBER
molecular dynamics software,

00:02:57.290 --> 00:03:00.470
where you can do a simulation
of the interaction of molecules

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:01.250
together.

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:07.355
And this chart shows you that
in some cases, using CPU nodes--

00:03:07.355 --> 00:03:10.190
up to 100 CPU nodes--

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:13.700
I'm sorry, 48
2-processor CPU nodes--

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:16.280
you can get up to
around 20 nanoseconds

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:17.810
of simulation per day.

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:20.690
So 20 nanoseconds everyday
takes a lot of days

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:22.610
to get up to a single
day of simulation.

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:25.250
With a GPU, you get 2 and
1/2 times that performance,

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:29.320
with three nodes of four GPUs.

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:31.150
And Nvidia was kind
enough to share

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:33.120
some data with us, our partner.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:36.280
They have mentioned that
GPU developer interest

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:38.110
is increasing substantially.

00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:41.050
We have over three
times as many developers

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:43.480
signed up now in
the past two years--

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:45.700
up to 400,000-- and
more than 20,000

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:49.550
organizations engage with Nvidia
for deep learning activities.

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:52.900
And you can see that the major
deep learning frameworks are

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:54.370
all GPU-enabled now--

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:56.410
accelerated by GPUs.

00:03:56.410 --> 00:03:59.239
So what are the things
you can do with a GPU?

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:01.780
Raise your hand if you've done
some of these things-- machine

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:02.504
learning?

00:04:02.504 --> 00:04:03.670
Who's done machine learning?

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:04.810
So a good number group.

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:07.420
Frame rendering
image composition?

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:08.320
A people here.

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:10.720
Physical simulation,
analysis, FEM?

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:12.290
A few people.

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:15.607
Real-time visual analytics
on a SQL database?

00:04:15.607 --> 00:04:16.690
You'll see that one today.

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:17.814
We're going to show a demo.

00:04:17.814 --> 00:04:20.680
How about faster-than-real-time
4K video encoding,

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:21.709
or transcoding?

00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:24.730
We've got someone here
that's excited about that.

00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:28.657
Sequence mapping, and
alignment-- or subsurface

00:04:28.657 --> 00:04:30.240
analysis for the oil
and gas industry?

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:32.406
I should see some hands
from Schlumberger over here.

00:04:32.406 --> 00:04:35.298
They'll be talking
about that later.

00:04:35.298 --> 00:04:36.842
So, just to give
you some examples--

00:04:36.842 --> 00:04:39.050
so we're going to be talking
about V-Ray today, where

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:41.630
we have more than 100 K80 GPUs.

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:45.980
That would be on the order of
500 teraflops of performance,

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:49.850
rendering massive
models in real time.

00:04:49.850 --> 00:04:52.676
And then we're going to
show a demo of MapD's

00:04:52.676 --> 00:04:54.050
real-time visual
analytics, where

00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:56.216
they use a parallel processing
power and high-memory

00:04:56.216 --> 00:05:01.670
bandwidth of the GPU to query
billions of rows at a time.

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:03.800
And I'd like to highlight
this quote, which

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:05.450
is also on our web page--

00:05:05.450 --> 00:05:11.540
that single high-mem 32 instance
with eight GPUs was almost 100

00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:12.140
times faster--

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:14.750
85 times faster--
than Impala running

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:17.400
on a cluster of six CPU nodes.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:21.250
So that's an example
of real value.

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:25.510
So, as you know, we have
K80s available today in beta.

00:05:25.510 --> 00:05:28.050
We've announced, and
will soon deliver,

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:32.070
S9300s from AMD and
Nvidia Tesla P100s--

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:34.740
a lot of enthusiasm for
the P100s coming out.

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:39.120
And right now they're available
in the US, Europe, and Asia.

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:41.850
And this, as I mentioned
before, offers teraflops

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:44.340
of performance per instance--

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:46.770
and especially for
machine learning,

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:51.170
engineering simulations,
and molecular modeling.

00:05:51.170 --> 00:05:52.990
You can attach, as
I mentioned also,

00:05:52.990 --> 00:05:55.870
eight GPUs per virtual machine.

00:05:55.870 --> 00:05:59.580
And you can do either one, two,
four, or eight simultaneously.

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:01.496
We have them in
three regions today.

00:06:01.496 --> 00:06:02.870
And then we have
another region--

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:06.601
US West-- coming online
within the next few weeks.

00:06:06.601 --> 00:06:08.600
One thing that people
really like about the GPUs

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:12.020
in Google Cloud is that we
offer essentially bare metal

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:13.070
performance.

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:15.230
We're offering them through
passthrough mode, which

00:06:15.230 --> 00:06:17.960
means they're directly
attached to the CPU

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:19.710
that your VM is running on.

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:20.810
So it's directly attached.

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:23.240
You get high performance.

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:26.040
And you can attach
to any machine type.

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:29.300
So you have a lot of
flexibility, as well.

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:32.090
And since we charge
only per minute,

00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:34.160
with a 10-minute
minimum, you can

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:37.220
choose a GPU that
best fits your wallet

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:40.050
and, also, pay only
for what you use.

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:42.080
You don't have to
worry so much about

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:45.584
if you're using it for a full
hour or a fraction of an hour.

00:06:45.584 --> 00:06:48.000
And then you don't have to
make a capital investment, too,

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:49.670
which is a huge advantage.

00:06:49.670 --> 00:06:51.630
I think pricing is
available online.

00:06:51.630 --> 00:06:53.210
But I thought I'd
mentioned it here--

00:06:53.210 --> 00:06:57.440
that it's $0.70 per GPU per
hour for the K80s in the US.

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:03.710
And then Europe and Asia are
each $0.77 per hour per GPU.

00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:05.530
Now here's the nice thing is--

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:07.610
again, people raised
their hand earlier.

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:10.600
If you've installed
a GPU before,

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:13.420
you know it's a lot
of work to do that--

00:07:13.420 --> 00:07:15.520
buying the hardware,
waiting for it to arrive,

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:18.070
buying the system that
surrounds that, the networking,

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:20.456
putting it all
together, assembling it.

00:07:20.456 --> 00:07:22.330
It doesn't work the
first time-- why is that?

00:07:22.330 --> 00:07:23.800
Is the GPU bad?

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:26.320
Did I seat the card
correctly in the board?

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:27.820
It's really a lot of--

00:07:27.820 --> 00:07:29.140
it's a challenge.

00:07:29.140 --> 00:07:31.670
And you have to have
the physical space.

00:07:31.670 --> 00:07:35.130
And the other thing
is hardware does fail.

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:37.420
Who manages the
fleet to make sure

00:07:37.420 --> 00:07:39.940
that if the hardware fails,
it gets replaced and fixed.

00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:42.500
But I think one of the biggest
things is power and cooling.

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:46.390
So if you're thinking about
running 100 GPUs somewhere,

00:07:46.390 --> 00:07:49.680
those GPUs put out a lot of heat
into your data center, right,

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:51.610
or into the closet
you're putting them in.

00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:54.110
And so you have to be careful
or you might install more GPUs

00:07:54.110 --> 00:07:56.485
than your cooling system can
handle and, actually, not be

00:07:56.485 --> 00:07:58.180
able to run at the
high performance.

00:07:58.180 --> 00:07:59.772
We've taken care
of that for you.

00:07:59.772 --> 00:08:01.230
So if you use GPUs
in the card, you

00:08:01.230 --> 00:08:03.410
don't have to worry about
any of these things.

00:08:03.410 --> 00:08:05.640
Just to step you through
what it takes to provision

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:07.160
a GPU in the cloud.

00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:09.700
I was going to suggest that
someone get out their computer

00:08:09.700 --> 00:08:12.760
and start putting together
a GPU machine right now.

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:15.320
You can start ordering
online to order a GPU

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:16.570
and see how long it takes you.

00:08:16.570 --> 00:08:18.028
In the meantime,
we'll have a race.

00:08:18.028 --> 00:08:20.320
We'll see how quickly we
can get one up in the cloud.

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:21.740
I think I'm going to win.

00:08:21.740 --> 00:08:23.000
So you go to the GPU--

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:24.250
to the console.

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:26.530
And you choose the
Compute Engine tab.

00:08:26.530 --> 00:08:28.990
And click on the
Create Instance button.

00:08:28.990 --> 00:08:30.670
And then you can
choose Customize--

00:08:30.670 --> 00:08:32.710
that's where the GPUs
are hidden right now.

00:08:32.710 --> 00:08:34.309
Once you choose
Customize, you'll

00:08:34.309 --> 00:08:36.100
see that you can choose
the number of cores

00:08:36.100 --> 00:08:37.308
and how much memory you want.

00:08:37.308 --> 00:08:40.140
And there's a little GPU link
you can click on underneath.

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:42.640
And you can choose the number
of GPUs you want and the type.

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:44.886
Of course, the type
right now is the K80.

00:08:44.886 --> 00:08:47.260
And the number-- you can choose
one, two, four, or eight,

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:48.710
as I mentioned before.

00:08:48.710 --> 00:08:50.732
Now if you prefer to
use the command line,

00:08:50.732 --> 00:08:52.690
here's an example command
line for provisioning

00:08:52.690 --> 00:08:54.070
a GPU instance.

00:08:54.070 --> 00:08:56.410
And the main thing
to pay attention to

00:08:56.410 --> 00:09:00.610
is the accelerator option,
with type and count

00:09:00.610 --> 00:09:02.490
to say what you want.

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:05.890
So either one will
get you a GPU.

00:09:05.890 --> 00:09:09.310
Now I want to mention, too, GPUs
don't support live migration.

00:09:09.310 --> 00:09:11.980
Because we're offering
them in passthrough mode,

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:13.960
to get the high performance,
we don't actually

00:09:13.960 --> 00:09:17.560
have the capacity to capture
the information in the GPU--

00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:19.900
the current state of the
GPU in the memory, and move

00:09:19.900 --> 00:09:21.880
that over to another
physical GPU.

00:09:21.880 --> 00:09:24.610
So when it's time
for maintenance,

00:09:24.610 --> 00:09:27.100
we notify you, give you
one hour notification.

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:29.190
And the GPU terminates
one hour after we give you

00:09:29.190 --> 00:09:30.430
a notification.

00:09:30.430 --> 00:09:32.800
And you can-- we'll give
you some support for that.

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:35.830
If you watch the
VM metadata, you

00:09:35.830 --> 00:09:38.140
can see that if you
run this command

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:40.650
you'll get a none if
maintenance is not scheduled.

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:43.150
Or you'll get a time stamp that
says when the maintenance is

00:09:43.150 --> 00:09:43.871
going to happen.

00:09:43.871 --> 00:09:45.370
And so, based on
the time stamp, you

00:09:45.370 --> 00:09:48.100
can either reboot right
away and get a new machine.

00:09:48.100 --> 00:09:50.020
Or you can check-point
what you're working on,

00:09:50.020 --> 00:09:53.380
save it, and bring it
back up in a new instance.

00:09:53.380 --> 00:09:57.880
And I should have mentioned that
the information about how to do

00:09:57.880 --> 00:10:01.150
that is online in this link.

00:10:01.150 --> 00:10:02.850
And then, if you're
using TensorFlow,

00:10:02.850 --> 00:10:03.808
we make it even easier.

00:10:03.808 --> 00:10:05.670
You can use TensorFlow
as supervisor.

00:10:05.670 --> 00:10:10.340
And that allows you to manage
banks of GPU-based VMs.

00:10:10.340 --> 00:10:13.430
And it handles both shutdowns
and crashes cleanly--

00:10:13.430 --> 00:10:15.490
easily resume, so you
don't lose any data.

00:10:15.490 --> 00:10:17.323
Because especially when
you're doing machine

00:10:17.323 --> 00:10:19.890
learning training, it might
run for weeks at a time.

00:10:19.890 --> 00:10:22.800
And you don't want to lose
your work due to a maintenance

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:24.090
event.

00:10:24.090 --> 00:10:25.830
So now I'd like
to invite Todd up

00:10:25.830 --> 00:10:29.760
on the stage to give a
demonstration-- introduce

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:31.378
himself and give
a demonstration.

00:10:31.378 --> 00:10:34.514
TODD MOSTAK: Thanks, John.

00:10:34.514 --> 00:10:36.170
Hey, everyone.

00:10:36.170 --> 00:10:38.180
Really excited to be
here today to tell you

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:41.570
a little bit about MapD, which
is a GPU accelerated database

00:10:41.570 --> 00:10:43.220
and visual analytics platform.

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:47.200
And we'd really like to thank
John and the Google Cloud team.

00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:48.860
We're tremendously
excited about what

00:10:48.860 --> 00:10:51.193
Google is bringing to market
here, in terms of their GPU

00:10:51.193 --> 00:10:52.070
instances.

00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:53.920
There's a lot of
flexibility for developers.

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:56.210
There's a lot of flexibility
for startups like us,

00:10:56.210 --> 00:10:59.750
to basically mix and match
components, and deliver

00:10:59.750 --> 00:11:02.330
the perfect system.

00:11:02.330 --> 00:11:04.100
So just a little bit about us--

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:08.760
we are a series A startup;
have some great investors--

00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:11.750
including Google,
as well as Nvidia.

00:11:11.750 --> 00:11:13.370
We've won some awards.

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:15.290
We were Garnter Cool
Vendor last year.

00:11:15.290 --> 00:11:17.870
And have had a good
set of early customers,

00:11:17.870 --> 00:11:20.510
including Verizon Wireless, who
I'll be speaking with at Strata

00:11:20.510 --> 00:11:21.009
next week.

00:11:24.070 --> 00:11:26.010
So everybody knows
that organizations

00:11:26.010 --> 00:11:27.870
are awash in data.

00:11:27.870 --> 00:11:30.660
So they're saying that
data growth is actually

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:33.630
40% year-over-year for
your average organization.

00:11:33.630 --> 00:11:35.160
And storage is not
the issue, right?

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:37.050
So people can put
the data on disk.

00:11:37.050 --> 00:11:40.950
They're building massive data
lakes, massive Hadoop clusters.

00:11:40.950 --> 00:11:42.516
Storage is not the issue.

00:11:42.516 --> 00:11:44.890
The issue is when you actually
want to analyze this data.

00:11:44.890 --> 00:11:49.380
So if data is growing at
a 40% year-over-year rate,

00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:50.880
CPU compute is
actually only growing

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:52.770
at a 20% year-over-year rate.

00:11:52.770 --> 00:11:55.860
And so this compute gap
between the growth in data

00:11:55.860 --> 00:11:57.930
and the growth in
CPU is causing people

00:11:57.930 --> 00:12:00.030
to do all sorts of
unnatural things.

00:12:00.030 --> 00:12:03.090
People down-sample,
people pre-aggregate,

00:12:03.090 --> 00:12:05.730
people, basically, have to
deploy huge scale-out clusters

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:07.840
of hundreds, if not
thousands, of nodes,

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:10.080
with diminishing
returns of scale.

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:12.160
And so people are
at this impasse.

00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:13.410
And that's where GPUs come in.

00:12:13.410 --> 00:12:16.050
So I don't want to reiterate
what John has said,

00:12:16.050 --> 00:12:20.850
but GPUs are not only fast,
but their processing power is

00:12:20.850 --> 00:12:22.320
growing at a tremendous rate.

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:24.570
And not only is it keeping
up with the growth in data,

00:12:24.570 --> 00:12:27.720
but it's actually surpassing it.

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:29.850
So whether you measure
by compute bandwidth

00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:32.940
or by memory bandwidth,
you have a huge amount

00:12:32.940 --> 00:12:34.129
of throughput on GPUs.

00:12:34.129 --> 00:12:36.420
And I'd like to point out
that everybody pays attention

00:12:36.420 --> 00:12:37.380
to flops--

00:12:37.380 --> 00:12:39.364
and certainly GPUs
deliver there.

00:12:39.364 --> 00:12:41.280
Actually, if you look
at the memory bandwidth,

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:43.980
that's almost more important
for data analytics problems,

00:12:43.980 --> 00:12:49.030
where things are often
memory bandwidth-bound.

00:12:49.030 --> 00:12:50.620
So that's where MapD comes in.

00:12:50.620 --> 00:12:53.080
So MapD was built from
the ground up to leverage

00:12:53.080 --> 00:12:55.460
the massive parallelism
and memory bandwidth

00:12:55.460 --> 00:12:59.740
of these graphics processing
units, to literally deliver

00:12:59.740 --> 00:13:04.210
100 plus x speedups over CPU
systems on a equal hardware

00:13:04.210 --> 00:13:05.560
cost basis--

00:13:05.560 --> 00:13:08.410
going back to that quote
that John showed earlier,

00:13:08.410 --> 00:13:10.260
comparing us to Impala.

00:13:10.260 --> 00:13:12.030
And so you can run
these crazy fast SQL

00:13:12.030 --> 00:13:13.630
queries over billions of rows--

00:13:13.630 --> 00:13:15.790
subsecond-- allowing,
for the first time,

00:13:15.790 --> 00:13:18.465
truly interactive
exploration of big data sets.

00:13:18.465 --> 00:13:19.840
And because one
of the killer use

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:22.930
cases is so often slice
and dice visual analytics,

00:13:22.930 --> 00:13:26.740
we built MapD Immerse, which is
the web-based visual analytics

00:13:26.740 --> 00:13:29.710
engine, which not only leverages
the speed of these GPUs,

00:13:29.710 --> 00:13:32.170
but the ability of the
GPUs to render graphics.

00:13:32.170 --> 00:13:34.600
So we sort of use it
as a one-two punch,

00:13:34.600 --> 00:13:36.982
where we can run the SQL
query over billions of rows.

00:13:36.982 --> 00:13:39.190
And because you often don't
want to actually transmit

00:13:39.190 --> 00:13:40.700
all that data back
to the client,

00:13:40.700 --> 00:13:43.360
we can actually render it
in situ right on the GPUs,

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:46.720
using the graphics
processing pipeline,

00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:49.200
and render it in place and
send back a compressed PNG.

00:13:51.756 --> 00:13:53.880
So with that I'm going to
cut to a quick demo here.

00:13:59.976 --> 00:14:00.475
OK.

00:14:00.475 --> 00:14:02.061
Can everybody see my screen?

00:14:02.061 --> 00:14:02.560
Great.

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:05.740
So this is a relatively
sizable data set.

00:14:05.740 --> 00:14:08.350
This is 2 billion records.

00:14:08.350 --> 00:14:11.740
Basically all the ship AIS,
or ship transponder data,

00:14:11.740 --> 00:14:14.026
from throughout 2015.

00:14:14.026 --> 00:14:15.400
What's notable
here is that we're

00:14:15.400 --> 00:14:17.800
running on a single eight K80--

00:14:17.800 --> 00:14:20.860
or eight GPU 4K80--

00:14:20.860 --> 00:14:23.200
Google Compute instance.

00:14:23.200 --> 00:14:25.240
So if you tried to imagine
the amount of compute

00:14:25.240 --> 00:14:26.906
you would need to
bring to bear to allow

00:14:26.906 --> 00:14:29.200
interactive querying on this
dataset on a CPU cluster,

00:14:29.200 --> 00:14:32.655
it's pretty amazing we're
doing this on a single node.

00:14:32.655 --> 00:14:34.780
So you'll see all kinds of
different metrics here--

00:14:34.780 --> 00:14:38.410
number of unique ships;
histogram of vessel link

00:14:38.410 --> 00:14:40.990
destinations; here's
actually the ship ID,

00:14:40.990 --> 00:14:44.830
so we can drill down to
individual ship; speed,

00:14:44.830 --> 00:14:47.540
vessel type, time.

00:14:47.540 --> 00:14:49.570
And then, last off,
you have this map,

00:14:49.570 --> 00:14:52.690
which is actually all the points
color-coded by the vessel type.

00:14:52.690 --> 00:14:55.210
So this is an example of
MapD's back end rendering

00:14:55.210 --> 00:14:57.454
capability, what we call
our Iris rendering engine.

00:14:57.454 --> 00:14:59.620
You wouldn't want to actually
send 2 billion records

00:14:59.620 --> 00:15:01.175
from your server to your client.

00:15:01.175 --> 00:15:03.550
So we actually render and
rasterize that on our back end,

00:15:03.550 --> 00:15:06.850
on top of the SQL query,
almost as a visual UTF.

00:15:06.850 --> 00:15:10.460
And then spit out a PNG, an
interactive PNG, to the client.

00:15:10.460 --> 00:15:13.580
So the cool thing here is that,
actually, as I zoom in here,

00:15:13.580 --> 00:15:15.820
you're going to notice
that, basically--

00:15:15.820 --> 00:15:17.770
I'm going to go
into the Bay Area--

00:15:17.770 --> 00:15:20.655
everything updates in real time.

00:15:20.655 --> 00:15:22.280
And, actually, each
one of these charts

00:15:22.280 --> 00:15:24.320
is hitting the back
end with a query

00:15:24.320 --> 00:15:27.125
that's probably responding in
about 30 or 40 milliseconds.

00:15:27.125 --> 00:15:29.000
And that's why we can
be so interactive, even

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:31.760
at this scale, even on a single
node, because we're leveraging

00:15:31.760 --> 00:15:34.093
the power of these GPUs to,
basically, brute force these

00:15:34.093 --> 00:15:36.710
queries, and answer them
in a fraction of the time

00:15:36.710 --> 00:15:39.800
you could on a CPU-based system.

00:15:39.800 --> 00:15:42.260
So, as I zoom in here,
I can look, you know--

00:15:42.260 --> 00:15:46.070
what if I actually wanted
to see the fastest vessels?

00:15:46.070 --> 00:15:49.430
So I'm going to kind of
cross filter here and look

00:15:49.430 --> 00:15:51.890
at a number of records
here where people

00:15:51.890 --> 00:15:54.189
are going at about 20 knots.

00:15:54.189 --> 00:15:55.730
And then I can see
all these records.

00:15:55.730 --> 00:15:58.715
I'll notice, first off, that--

00:15:58.715 --> 00:16:00.800
if I increase this
even farther--

00:16:00.800 --> 00:16:04.570
most of the fastest boats
are actually passenger boats.

00:16:04.570 --> 00:16:07.150
So I could drill down
into that and we'll

00:16:07.150 --> 00:16:08.590
see these passenger boats.

00:16:08.590 --> 00:16:13.230
And then I could actually
find an individual boat here,

00:16:13.230 --> 00:16:16.270
and drill down and, actually--

00:16:16.270 --> 00:16:18.820
we could even zoom in and
see their exact tracks

00:16:18.820 --> 00:16:21.460
across the Bay Area here.

00:16:21.460 --> 00:16:25.870
So it's a pretty fun data set.

00:16:25.870 --> 00:16:27.650
And you can, again,
get very granular.

00:16:27.650 --> 00:16:30.010
So if we actually go
off and look, basically,

00:16:30.010 --> 00:16:32.200
out into the Bay,
you can actually

00:16:32.200 --> 00:16:33.520
see where the tankers sit.

00:16:33.520 --> 00:16:37.585
And they circle here and they
moor, which is pretty cool.

00:16:37.585 --> 00:16:40.162
I'm going to quickly
show you one other thing.

00:16:40.162 --> 00:16:41.120
How am I doing on time?

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:42.870
Let's see.

00:16:42.870 --> 00:16:43.370
OK.

00:16:43.370 --> 00:16:45.000
Just two more minutes.

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:47.970
So we not only built MapD, or
our Immerse visual analytics

00:16:47.970 --> 00:16:49.890
platform, to be
fast, but we also

00:16:49.890 --> 00:16:52.000
built it to be very easy to use.

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:53.970
So what if I wanted to
explore the relationship

00:16:53.970 --> 00:16:56.634
between speed--

00:16:56.634 --> 00:16:57.300
let's see, here.

00:16:57.300 --> 00:17:00.400
I'm going to go to scatterplot.

00:17:00.400 --> 00:17:04.720
What if we wanted to look at
the relationship between vessel

00:17:04.720 --> 00:17:05.710
length--

00:17:05.710 --> 00:17:07.690
and we'll put that
on the x-axis--

00:17:07.690 --> 00:17:11.745
and speed, which is SOG here.

00:17:11.745 --> 00:17:13.369
So we immediately
have the scatterplot.

00:17:13.369 --> 00:17:14.994
And, again, this is
actually leveraging

00:17:14.994 --> 00:17:17.810
the power of the GPUs to
do this on the back end.

00:17:20.899 --> 00:17:23.190
And here, for good measure,
I'm actually going to color

00:17:23.190 --> 00:17:24.119
by the type of boat.

00:17:28.349 --> 00:17:33.990
So immediately here you can see
that the bigger the boat gets--

00:17:33.990 --> 00:17:36.150
the longer the boat
gets-- the faster it goes,

00:17:36.150 --> 00:17:38.130
except the smaller boats--

00:17:38.130 --> 00:17:39.750
the slow ones are
the tug boats here--

00:17:39.750 --> 00:17:43.170
but the longer ones are
passenger and other types

00:17:43.170 --> 00:17:43.997
of ships.

00:17:43.997 --> 00:17:45.330
So I could easily build on this.

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:48.750
If I wanted to, I could add
another chart and cross-filter.

00:17:48.750 --> 00:17:51.160
But it's very interactive
and easy to use.

00:17:51.160 --> 00:17:53.700
And this kind of interactive
data exploration at scale

00:17:53.700 --> 00:17:56.430
is something that
MapD really excels at.

00:17:56.430 --> 00:17:58.220
So, thank you.

00:17:58.220 --> 00:17:58.882
Good job.

00:17:58.882 --> 00:18:01.342
