00:18:49.540 --> 00:18:52.420
So Schlumberger-- very
briefly, what do we do?

00:18:52.420 --> 00:18:56.410
We provide insight
into subsurface--

00:18:56.410 --> 00:19:01.330
everything below the earth
and the layers and structures

00:19:01.330 --> 00:19:04.240
and where the hydrocarbons are.

00:19:06.770 --> 00:19:09.260
This is the sort
of services that we

00:19:09.260 --> 00:19:10.350
provide to our customers.

00:19:10.350 --> 00:19:12.920
We are technology company.

00:19:12.920 --> 00:19:16.250
And so high-performance
computing, especially,

00:19:16.250 --> 00:19:18.590
is quite relevant
to our one service

00:19:18.590 --> 00:19:21.980
that I'm going to talk about,
which is the oil exploration.

00:19:21.980 --> 00:19:24.350
And we call it, you
know, seismic processing,

00:19:24.350 --> 00:19:26.390
seismic acquisition.

00:19:26.390 --> 00:19:26.890
All right.

00:19:26.890 --> 00:19:29.560
What's interesting for us,
as computer scientists,

00:19:29.560 --> 00:19:32.650
is the volume of data that we
acquire, the volume of data

00:19:32.650 --> 00:19:33.850
that we need to process.

00:19:33.850 --> 00:19:36.880
And for that we have massive,
high-performance computing

00:19:36.880 --> 00:19:39.070
centers around the
world, just dedicated

00:19:39.070 --> 00:19:40.680
for seismic processing.

00:19:40.680 --> 00:19:43.620
These centers have been--

00:19:43.620 --> 00:19:46.780
they hold everything from
building to [INAUDIBLE] space

00:19:46.780 --> 00:19:49.840
to CPUs, GPUs, storage.

00:19:49.840 --> 00:19:53.140
And we've been doing that
for quite a number of years,

00:19:53.140 --> 00:19:54.550
quite successfully.

00:19:54.550 --> 00:19:57.250
So you can look at the
volumes of the amount of data

00:19:57.250 --> 00:20:00.490
that we acquire and amount of
data that we have to process.

00:20:00.490 --> 00:20:07.180
It requires-- so it requires
a lot of computing power.

00:20:07.180 --> 00:20:10.570
And let me talk about
the seismic exploration

00:20:10.570 --> 00:20:13.960
in a very brief two steps.

00:20:13.960 --> 00:20:16.760
Step number one--
we acquire data.

00:20:16.760 --> 00:20:21.340
And, as you can see, we acquire
a large amount of data per job,

00:20:21.340 --> 00:20:22.720
or per project.

00:20:22.720 --> 00:20:25.660
And we cover kilometers
and kilometers square--

00:20:25.660 --> 00:20:29.450
[INAUDIBLE] kilometers
of area around the globe.

00:20:29.450 --> 00:20:33.395
Step number two is
to process, to build

00:20:33.395 --> 00:20:35.780
this sub-surface
understanding of what's

00:20:35.780 --> 00:20:37.790
really going on down there.

00:20:37.790 --> 00:20:40.490
That requires massive
amount of computing--

00:20:40.490 --> 00:20:44.660
millions of cores,
millions of GPU-- well,

00:20:44.660 --> 00:20:47.540
millions of threads and GPUs.

00:20:47.540 --> 00:20:50.630
And it is the classic,
high-performance computing

00:20:50.630 --> 00:20:53.980
that we've been
doing for many years.

00:20:53.980 --> 00:20:58.040
So the question we start asking
ourselves a couple years ago--

00:20:58.040 --> 00:21:00.700
and prior to that,
perhaps, a little bit--

00:21:00.700 --> 00:21:06.340
can we do this massive parallel
computing in-cloud effectively

00:21:06.340 --> 00:21:07.310
for business?

00:21:07.310 --> 00:21:11.750
Can we do it for
all the workflows

00:21:11.750 --> 00:21:15.110
that we need to do in seismic,
not just one or two algorithms?

00:21:15.110 --> 00:21:19.920
And the answer
is, today, yes, we

00:21:19.920 --> 00:21:22.710
are doing commercial work
in Google Cloud using

00:21:22.710 --> 00:21:24.060
computer [INAUDIBLE] with GPU.

00:21:24.060 --> 00:21:26.370
Without that, it would
not have been possible.

00:21:26.370 --> 00:21:27.420
How do we do this?

00:21:27.420 --> 00:21:31.320
I'm going to invite my
colleague, Carlos, to talk

00:21:31.320 --> 00:21:32.460
about that.

00:21:32.460 --> 00:21:34.061
Thank you.

00:21:34.061 --> 00:21:35.357
