00:21:35.357 --> 00:21:36.690
CARLOS BONETI: Thank you, Kanai.

00:21:45.709 --> 00:21:46.750
CARLOS BONETI: All right.

00:21:46.750 --> 00:21:50.870
So how do we do
seismic processing?

00:21:50.870 --> 00:21:55.880
We have strong requirements on
dynamic resource allocation,

00:21:55.880 --> 00:21:57.720
data management,
and fault tolerance.

00:21:57.720 --> 00:21:59.990
This is because of the nature
of our business, the way

00:21:59.990 --> 00:22:02.180
we do processing, right?

00:22:02.180 --> 00:22:04.640
And because of
these requirements,

00:22:04.640 --> 00:22:09.020
we try to avoid using MPI
or most of the classic HPC--

00:22:09.020 --> 00:22:11.540
high-performance
computing-- frameworks.

00:22:11.540 --> 00:22:15.920
So a few years back, we
built our own software stack

00:22:15.920 --> 00:22:18.140
for our own data centers.

00:22:18.140 --> 00:22:21.500
And that stack provides
a map-reduce-like kind

00:22:21.500 --> 00:22:24.570
of infrastructure that
introduced sufficient amounts

00:22:24.570 --> 00:22:28.970
of fault tolerance and
dynamic resource management

00:22:28.970 --> 00:22:31.480
and it contains-- so, for
doing this, it contains,

00:22:31.480 --> 00:22:35.270
basically, has its own custom
job scheduler and custom

00:22:35.270 --> 00:22:38.790
resource and data
management layers.

00:22:38.790 --> 00:22:41.120
As we brought that
software stack

00:22:41.120 --> 00:22:44.900
that we built for own data
centers into the cloud,

00:22:44.900 --> 00:22:46.790
some things were
familiar-- like,

00:22:46.790 --> 00:22:49.730
for instance, the
Nvidia K80s, right,

00:22:49.730 --> 00:22:52.940
which provided great
performance per dollar.

00:22:52.940 --> 00:22:57.540
And other things were
essentially opportunities.

00:22:57.540 --> 00:23:00.600
So some of those
opportunities were the ability

00:23:00.600 --> 00:23:03.720
to dynamically create
instances, dynamically adapt

00:23:03.720 --> 00:23:07.500
the VM's characteristics,
to match the performance

00:23:07.500 --> 00:23:09.600
requirements of the jobs.

00:23:09.600 --> 00:23:13.220
And some of those were--

00:23:13.220 --> 00:23:17.010
like, for instance, the needs
for monitoring and control

00:23:17.010 --> 00:23:21.660
were a opportunity disguised
as a challenge, right?

00:23:21.660 --> 00:23:25.800
So in the graph here,
you will notice--

00:23:25.800 --> 00:23:27.509
I know you can't quite
read the details--

00:23:27.509 --> 00:23:29.758
but you'll notice there are
three main layers on this,

00:23:29.758 --> 00:23:30.270
right?

00:23:30.270 --> 00:23:33.510
On the top layer, you
have a very small number

00:23:33.510 --> 00:23:36.780
of nodes, which
contain what I call

00:23:36.780 --> 00:23:39.390
the durable infrastructure.

00:23:39.390 --> 00:23:43.020
These are the pieces that make
the virtual data center work.

00:23:43.020 --> 00:23:47.010
It's a small number of nodes,
like the job controller.

00:23:47.010 --> 00:23:49.940
Now the middle layer is the
bulk of the data center,

00:23:49.940 --> 00:23:51.360
is the bulk of--

00:23:51.360 --> 00:23:52.960
are the compute nodes.

00:23:52.960 --> 00:23:55.380
These are what we call the
ephemeral infrastructure.

00:23:55.380 --> 00:23:58.320
And they basically live
for the length of a job.

00:23:58.320 --> 00:24:00.120
You create those
nodes on-demand--

00:24:00.120 --> 00:24:04.590
sized and dimensioned
for what your job needs.

00:24:04.590 --> 00:24:06.560
And the bottom of the
layer is basically

00:24:06.560 --> 00:24:08.460
composed of a parallel
file system that

00:24:08.460 --> 00:24:12.870
feeds the data through
those to the compute nodes

00:24:12.870 --> 00:24:16.050
and has a lifecycle that is
independent of the other two

00:24:16.050 --> 00:24:17.120
layers.

00:24:17.120 --> 00:24:20.940
Now, finally, what we have is--

00:24:20.940 --> 00:24:24.540
we call it the FAT
link-- it's a 20 GB link

00:24:24.540 --> 00:24:27.660
between our data center
in Houston and Google data

00:24:27.660 --> 00:24:29.190
centers.

00:24:29.190 --> 00:24:33.240
And that allows us to,
basically, upload--

00:24:33.240 --> 00:24:36.750
well, move data, the massive
amounts of data that we have,

00:24:36.750 --> 00:24:38.250
between Houston and--

00:24:38.250 --> 00:24:40.794
between our data center
and Google, right?

00:24:40.794 --> 00:24:42.960
And when I say a massive
amount of data-- basically,

00:24:42.960 --> 00:24:47.790
a job commonly requires 60 to
100 terabytes of input data

00:24:47.790 --> 00:24:48.530
to start running.

00:24:51.590 --> 00:24:55.070
So to give you an example--

00:24:55.070 --> 00:24:59.120
in this case here, we
have a single imaging job.

00:24:59.120 --> 00:25:01.310
So this is running
reverse-time migration

00:25:01.310 --> 00:25:02.390
and lasts for a few days.

00:25:02.390 --> 00:25:04.570
We run it last weekend.

00:25:04.570 --> 00:25:07.710
This was probably
about three days.

00:25:07.710 --> 00:25:12.540
And, as you can see in the
curve here on your right--

00:25:12.540 --> 00:25:17.820
on your left-- it
starts with 20 nodes

00:25:17.820 --> 00:25:20.320
and it acquires nodes
as it scales up.

00:25:20.320 --> 00:25:25.920
So at its peak, It has a little
bit more than 1,700 nodes.

00:25:25.920 --> 00:25:29.790
And then it ramps down
as it finishes the work

00:25:29.790 --> 00:25:30.990
to be done, right?

00:25:30.990 --> 00:25:33.600
So I really get
excited about this kind

00:25:33.600 --> 00:25:36.420
of problem because
of the massive scale

00:25:36.420 --> 00:25:37.980
of these computations.

00:25:37.980 --> 00:25:42.980
So, at the peak, we are
using about 7,000 GPUs.

00:25:42.980 --> 00:25:45.750
And they're using something
around 20,000 CPUs

00:25:45.750 --> 00:25:48.150
for that single job.

00:25:48.150 --> 00:25:51.551
Now this is not the only
job that we're running.

00:25:51.551 --> 00:25:52.050
Right?

00:25:52.050 --> 00:25:54.900
Oh, and by the way, we're
generating about 220 gigabytes

00:25:54.900 --> 00:25:58.290
per second FIO, combined.

00:25:58.290 --> 00:26:00.540
But, again, this is not the
single job we're running.

00:26:00.540 --> 00:26:03.090
And it's not unusual
that we run multiple jobs

00:26:03.090 --> 00:26:05.232
at any given time.

00:26:05.232 --> 00:26:07.440
As a matter of fact, if you
look at the other graph--

00:26:07.440 --> 00:26:08.340
the green one--

00:26:08.340 --> 00:26:13.560
you'll see the utilization
curve for what we are calling

00:26:13.560 --> 00:26:15.090
our virtual data center, right?

00:26:15.090 --> 00:26:16.770
So it's the footprint at Google.

00:26:16.770 --> 00:26:22.050
And as you see, you have periods
that have about 50 nodes.

00:26:22.050 --> 00:26:24.150
And they go from
50 to 5,000 nodes

00:26:24.150 --> 00:26:26.940
within two or three hours--

00:26:26.940 --> 00:26:29.170
which, well, I find
it's very cool.

00:26:32.410 --> 00:26:35.500
So, to conclude, I
would like to reveal

00:26:35.500 --> 00:26:37.960
some of the
opportunities we found,

00:26:37.960 --> 00:26:41.350
which was the ability
to basically use

00:26:41.350 --> 00:26:43.540
the GPUs that are
familiar to us,

00:26:43.540 --> 00:26:45.790
that provide great performance.

00:26:45.790 --> 00:26:48.820
And the ability to
resize and dynamically

00:26:48.820 --> 00:26:51.640
create and delete VMs on demand.

00:26:51.640 --> 00:26:53.330
So we create-- as
a matter of fact,

00:26:53.330 --> 00:26:56.860
I think we create, usually,
a few thousands VMs per day,

00:26:56.860 --> 00:26:59.560
create and delete.

00:26:59.560 --> 00:27:02.920
And that comes with a price of--

00:27:02.920 --> 00:27:05.590
you have to be obsessed
about organizing, monitoring

00:27:05.590 --> 00:27:10.030
and control your center.

00:27:10.030 --> 00:27:12.320
But, by leveraging
these opportunities,

00:27:12.320 --> 00:27:14.925
we have successfully deployed
a virtual data center--

00:27:14.925 --> 00:27:17.050
what we call a virtual data
center at Google, which

00:27:17.050 --> 00:27:20.830
uses thousands of CPUs
and GPUs everyday, and do

00:27:20.830 --> 00:27:28.568
massive seismic processing for
production workloads today.

00:27:28.568 --> 00:27:30.014
Thank you.

00:27:30.014 --> 00:27:31.950
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:31.950 --> 00:27:33.717
- You can introduce him now.

00:27:33.717 --> 00:27:35.060
No?

00:27:35.060 --> 00:27:35.657
What's that?

00:27:35.657 --> 00:27:36.643
[INAUDIBLE]

00:27:37.143 --> 00:27:38.351
ADRIAN GRAHAM: No, it's okay.

00:27:38.351 --> 00:27:40.210
I got it.

00:27:40.210 --> 00:27:41.190
No, I don't need--

00:27:41.190 --> 00:27:42.270
I clicked myself.

00:27:42.270 --> 00:27:42.840
Hey.

00:27:42.840 --> 00:27:44.890
How you doing, everybody?

00:27:44.890 --> 00:27:47.090
Everybody can hear
me in the back?

00:27:47.090 --> 00:27:48.940
Mic's OK?

00:27:48.940 --> 00:27:49.570
Excuse me.

00:27:49.570 --> 00:27:51.580
Sorry.

00:27:51.580 --> 00:27:52.660
My name's Adrian Graham.

00:27:52.660 --> 00:27:56.152
I'm a Cloud Solutions
Architect at Google,

00:27:56.152 --> 00:27:58.360
in the Venice office-- that's
Venice, California, not

00:27:58.360 --> 00:27:59.500
Venice, Italy.

00:27:59.500 --> 00:28:02.442
Although the food in the
office is still pretty good.

00:28:02.442 --> 00:28:04.900
And I come from a background--
a visual effects background.

00:28:04.900 --> 00:28:08.140
I spent 20 years as a pipeline
TD, and as an effects artist,

00:28:08.140 --> 00:28:10.885
at companies like
ILM, Digital Domain,

00:28:10.885 --> 00:28:12.010
Double Negative in London--

00:28:12.010 --> 00:28:13.830
I worked on some big films.

00:28:13.830 --> 00:28:15.580
And rendering is one
of the big components

00:28:15.580 --> 00:28:16.990
of working on any film.

00:28:16.990 --> 00:28:18.865
And that's what we're
going to look at today.

00:28:18.865 --> 00:28:22.010
We're going to look at what a
renderer can do in the cloud,

00:28:22.010 --> 00:28:24.370
and what it can do,
furthermore, on the GPU.

00:28:24.370 --> 00:28:25.900
So what does a renderer need?

00:28:25.900 --> 00:28:28.600
What is-- the role in
the pipeline of somebody

00:28:28.600 --> 00:28:31.150
who's a renderer, or who
performs rendering actions,

00:28:31.150 --> 00:28:32.260
is a lighter.

00:28:32.260 --> 00:28:33.760
And they take all
of the assets, all

00:28:33.760 --> 00:28:35.890
of the massive
amounts of data that's

00:28:35.890 --> 00:28:40.120
generated by modelers and
texture artists and animators--

00:28:40.120 --> 00:28:41.640
and those are simple assets--

00:28:41.640 --> 00:28:45.070
the complex assets are
things like simulation data,

00:28:45.070 --> 00:28:48.610
and complex character rigs,
and massive environments,

00:28:48.610 --> 00:28:50.950
like if you have to lay
out an entire city--

00:28:50.950 --> 00:28:53.440
the island of Manhattan
could be all completely

00:28:53.440 --> 00:28:54.700
virtually generated.

00:28:54.700 --> 00:28:56.410
And what do you do
with all that data?

00:28:56.410 --> 00:28:58.840
And how do you move it
around and shift it?

00:28:58.840 --> 00:29:02.215
So the render-- the lighting
artist will take all this data

00:29:02.215 --> 00:29:04.090
and they'll make a
beautiful image out of it.

00:29:04.090 --> 00:29:04.930
And they'll use a renderer.

00:29:04.930 --> 00:29:07.096
Today we're going to look
at a renderer called V-Ray

00:29:07.096 --> 00:29:08.990
by Chaos Group.

00:29:08.990 --> 00:29:11.710
But to step back, what does a
lighter need to do their job?

00:29:11.710 --> 00:29:14.140
They need a very
powerful workstation--

00:29:14.140 --> 00:29:14.890
not a laptop.

00:29:14.890 --> 00:29:20.170
You need multicore, good GPUs,
lots of memory, lots of RAM,

00:29:20.170 --> 00:29:21.760
and lots of disk storage--

00:29:21.760 --> 00:29:24.070
all these assets
that are generated

00:29:24.070 --> 00:29:26.980
prior to the lighter
getting their hands on them

00:29:26.980 --> 00:29:29.110
take up massive
amounts of space.

00:29:29.110 --> 00:29:33.850
And as the data's coming
in, the data's going out,

00:29:33.850 --> 00:29:35.230
a lighter is generating frames.

00:29:35.230 --> 00:29:36.700
We don't render movie files.

00:29:36.700 --> 00:29:38.320
We render individual frames--

00:29:38.320 --> 00:29:40.810
24 frames in a second--
that's 24 images.

00:29:40.810 --> 00:29:43.210
And they could be HD
res, they could be 4K,

00:29:43.210 --> 00:29:45.400
they could be 8K, that
could be stereo pairs.

00:29:45.400 --> 00:29:48.100
Massive amounts are required.

00:29:48.100 --> 00:29:49.300
You need licensing.

00:29:49.300 --> 00:29:51.091
You need to be able to
license the software

00:29:51.091 --> 00:29:54.040
on the workstation, as
well as the render boxes.

00:29:54.040 --> 00:29:56.510
You need color accuracy,
so that everybody's

00:29:56.510 --> 00:29:57.750
seeing the same picture.

00:29:57.750 --> 00:30:00.250
And, of course, you need as
many render workers as possible.

00:30:00.250 --> 00:30:02.020
Now that's the
classic description

00:30:02.020 --> 00:30:04.700
of a lighting pipeline.

00:30:04.700 --> 00:30:06.580
Today we're going
to look at a project

00:30:06.580 --> 00:30:08.080
by our friend Kevin Margo.

00:30:08.080 --> 00:30:10.840
Kevin is a-- let me
just play these guys--

00:30:10.840 --> 00:30:18.069
Kevin is a VFX supervisor at
Blur Studios in Los Angeles.

00:30:18.069 --> 00:30:20.110
And Kevin did this short
film called "Construct,"

00:30:20.110 --> 00:30:22.320
or "Construct," however
you want to say it.

00:30:22.320 --> 00:30:24.100
It's about this robot
construction worker,

00:30:24.100 --> 00:30:28.060
who shows up on the site one
day to find something horrible

00:30:28.060 --> 00:30:28.994
is going on.

00:30:28.994 --> 00:30:30.160
You could see it on YouTube.

00:30:30.160 --> 00:30:32.050
It's a really great short film.

00:30:32.050 --> 00:30:35.534
But on the right hand
side of your screen,

00:30:35.534 --> 00:30:36.700
you're seeing the making of.

00:30:36.700 --> 00:30:41.057
Now Kevin had some pretty--
he's this visionary director.

00:30:41.057 --> 00:30:42.640
He's got some pretty
interesting ideas

00:30:42.640 --> 00:30:45.760
about how to do virtual sets
and virtual motion capture

00:30:45.760 --> 00:30:46.535
sessions.

00:30:46.535 --> 00:30:48.160
And you could see
the guy in the suit--

00:30:48.160 --> 00:30:52.030
Darren Ross-- he is wearing
a motion capture suit

00:30:52.030 --> 00:30:56.530
with points on it that are being
tracked by a bunch of cameras.

00:30:56.530 --> 00:31:00.250
That data is being fed
into 3D Studio Max,

00:31:00.250 --> 00:31:03.190
which is the 3D application,
and being rendered in real time

00:31:03.190 --> 00:31:04.180
by V-Ray--

00:31:04.180 --> 00:31:06.970
V-Ray RT, in this case.

00:31:06.970 --> 00:31:10.180
In the video, you'll also
see Boris, right, Simandoff,

00:31:10.180 --> 00:31:12.310
who's in the audience
today with us.

00:31:12.310 --> 00:31:16.560
Boris is the director of
V-Ray Cloud and IT at Chaos.

00:31:16.560 --> 00:31:17.570
Did I get that right?

00:31:17.570 --> 00:31:18.070
Thanks.

00:31:20.350 --> 00:31:23.530
There's also some pretty
revolutionary cinematography

00:31:23.530 --> 00:31:25.824
techniques that
Kevin has figured out

00:31:25.824 --> 00:31:27.490
and that they're using
a virtual camera.

00:31:27.490 --> 00:31:28.960
There's no real-world camera.

00:31:28.960 --> 00:31:31.720
It's CG-- it's a
camera that is simply--

00:31:31.720 --> 00:31:33.580
that rig that you
saw with the screen,

00:31:33.580 --> 00:31:35.117
tracked by the director.

00:31:35.117 --> 00:31:37.200
So that you could really
visualize what's going on

00:31:37.200 --> 00:31:38.074
and what it's going--

00:31:38.074 --> 00:31:41.766
the final result is
going to look like.

00:31:41.766 --> 00:31:43.390
That also means that
you're not storing

00:31:43.390 --> 00:31:44.389
massive amounts of data.

00:31:44.389 --> 00:31:46.340
You're only storing
the positional data

00:31:46.340 --> 00:31:50.100
for the camera itself.

00:31:50.100 --> 00:31:53.435
Now this is the on-set
preview on the right-hand side

00:31:53.435 --> 00:31:54.310
that you're watching.

00:31:54.310 --> 00:31:57.300
But on the left-hand side,
that's the final render.

00:31:57.300 --> 00:32:00.196
The final render,
in this case, they

00:32:00.196 --> 00:32:02.070
tried a bunch of different
ways to render it.

00:32:02.070 --> 00:32:05.400
They used CPUs-- each frame
took about two to four hours

00:32:05.400 --> 00:32:06.480
to render.

00:32:06.480 --> 00:32:08.010
And they tried GPUs.

00:32:08.010 --> 00:32:10.470
And this is a couple of
years ago, so there was--

00:32:10.470 --> 00:32:13.053
the setups were a lot different
and they didn't have the power

00:32:13.053 --> 00:32:14.460
that we have on GCP.

00:32:14.460 --> 00:32:18.430
And those renders took
about 18 minutes or so.

00:32:18.430 --> 00:32:20.990
So what if we could do this
on a Google Cloud Platform?

00:32:23.690 --> 00:32:26.720
And let me stop these guys.

00:32:26.720 --> 00:32:30.830
Well, it just so
happens that we can.

00:32:30.830 --> 00:32:36.860
So, before I start, I'm
running a remote workstation.

00:32:36.860 --> 00:32:38.540
I'm not running
Windows on my Mac.

00:32:38.540 --> 00:32:41.600
I'm running a remote
workstation in Us Central,

00:32:41.600 --> 00:32:45.440
which is in Iowa, which is
about 1,500 miles away--

00:32:45.440 --> 00:32:48.740
40 millisecond, round-trip ping.

00:32:48.740 --> 00:32:52.250
And this is the full
scene that you see there.

00:32:52.250 --> 00:32:59.580
And I am going to track around
a bit and show that-- oh,

00:32:59.580 --> 00:33:04.720
I'm sorry, my mouse wheel is
a little bit wonky there--

00:33:04.720 --> 00:33:05.920
but I'm going to zoom out.

00:33:05.920 --> 00:33:09.910
This is operating over internet,
so it's a little bit slow.

00:33:09.910 --> 00:33:12.250
But the render is happening
almost in real time.

00:33:12.250 --> 00:33:14.230
And you've got to
remember how much data is

00:33:14.230 --> 00:33:16.930
being generated in this scene.

00:33:16.930 --> 00:33:19.520
Each of these-- it's doing
geometry substitution

00:33:19.520 --> 00:33:20.020
on the fly.

00:33:20.020 --> 00:33:23.350
Each of those red pyramids
is being substituted

00:33:23.350 --> 00:33:26.110
for a full-resolution tree.

00:33:26.110 --> 00:33:28.000
And you could zoom
in to the forest.

00:33:28.000 --> 00:33:30.276
You could select a single
object and zoom in on it.

00:33:30.276 --> 00:33:31.150
There's the dumpster.

00:33:34.210 --> 00:33:35.690
I can go back to--

00:33:35.690 --> 00:33:37.790
let me just go
back to the robots.

00:33:37.790 --> 00:33:40.250
And the whole idea
behind this is not just

00:33:40.250 --> 00:33:41.670
to move around your scene.

00:33:41.670 --> 00:33:44.750
But you can change lighting,
and you can change animation,

00:33:44.750 --> 00:33:48.600
and you can change
shading qualities.

00:33:48.600 --> 00:33:50.940
Step back one more--

00:33:50.940 --> 00:33:55.760
just want to make this a little
smaller so I can see my robots.

00:33:55.760 --> 00:33:57.610
And there we are.

00:34:02.500 --> 00:34:04.270
My robots seemed to
have disappeared.

00:34:04.270 --> 00:34:06.030
But you guys get the idea.

00:34:06.030 --> 00:34:08.530
Oh, there we are.

00:34:08.530 --> 00:34:10.370
Let me just show the entire--

00:34:10.370 --> 00:34:11.405
these live demos.

00:34:15.190 --> 00:34:15.690
All right.

00:34:15.690 --> 00:34:20.510
So I have hosed my demo.

00:34:20.510 --> 00:34:24.679
But that gave you an idea of
what's going on in the cloud.

00:34:24.679 --> 00:34:25.489
Come see me after.

00:34:25.489 --> 00:34:28.610
I could get it running again.

00:34:28.610 --> 00:34:32.288
So how do we do this?

00:34:32.288 --> 00:34:35.320
This is sort of
a little bit more

00:34:35.320 --> 00:34:38.469
complex architectural
diagram of how we did this,

00:34:38.469 --> 00:34:41.050
what we're doing right now.

00:34:41.050 --> 00:34:44.260
For example, we're not doing
any asset management database.

00:34:44.260 --> 00:34:47.440
We're not syncing our cloud
directory user database.

00:34:47.440 --> 00:34:49.540
But we're using
something called--

00:34:49.540 --> 00:34:51.909
we're running over
Teradici PCoIP, which

00:34:51.909 --> 00:34:56.560
is PC over IP, which is a remote
desktop protocol developed

00:34:56.560 --> 00:34:58.300
by a company called Teradici.

00:34:58.300 --> 00:35:00.730
And you can run this
on a piece of hardware.

00:35:00.730 --> 00:35:04.030
This is zero client, which
just has a bunch of video outs

00:35:04.030 --> 00:35:07.192
and an ethernet
port and some USBs.

00:35:07.192 --> 00:35:08.650
And there's also
a software client,

00:35:08.650 --> 00:35:11.170
which is what I'm
running on my Mac here.

00:35:11.170 --> 00:35:12.640
There's one for Windows.

00:35:12.640 --> 00:35:14.560
And there is one
for iOS devices,

00:35:14.560 --> 00:35:17.320
as well, which is pretty cool.

00:35:17.320 --> 00:35:19.690
Boris has it running
on his phone.

00:35:19.690 --> 00:35:20.530
It's not even iOS.

00:35:20.530 --> 00:35:21.380
It's Android, right?

00:35:21.380 --> 00:35:23.290
Or running in a browser.

00:35:23.290 --> 00:35:26.530
We are also running
a remote machine

00:35:26.530 --> 00:35:29.650
running 3D Studio Max and a
bunch of render instances.

00:35:29.650 --> 00:35:32.320
The render instances are
running Linux CentOS 7.

00:35:32.320 --> 00:35:36.610
And, as you saw, unfortunately,
the interactive remote desktop

00:35:36.610 --> 00:35:37.960
machine is running Windows--

00:35:37.960 --> 00:35:38.820
an ancient version.

00:35:38.820 --> 00:35:39.989
I apologize for that.

00:35:39.989 --> 00:35:42.280
There are some plugins that
I don't want to talk about.

00:35:42.280 --> 00:35:43.696
And then there's
a license server.

00:35:43.696 --> 00:35:45.130
I'm running a Linux
license server

00:35:45.130 --> 00:35:48.130
that's feeding licenses to
both the render instances

00:35:48.130 --> 00:35:49.780
and the remote desktop.

00:35:49.780 --> 00:35:52.060
And, of course, we're
rendering using V-Ray.

00:35:52.060 --> 00:35:56.790
And those are licensed
via the cloud as well.

00:35:56.790 --> 00:35:58.820
So how do we set all this up?

00:35:58.820 --> 00:36:01.364
Quite simply, first, we want
to create a workstation.

00:36:01.364 --> 00:36:02.780
And there's three
important points

00:36:02.780 --> 00:36:05.360
I want to indicate
on this slide here--

00:36:05.360 --> 00:36:07.520
is that, number one,
we choose our zone.

00:36:07.520 --> 00:36:10.190
And, as John said
earlier, it's going

00:36:10.190 --> 00:36:12.680
to be available in a
number of different zones.

00:36:12.680 --> 00:36:15.380
West-- US West is
coming up very soon.

00:36:15.380 --> 00:36:19.130
Had it already been running,
we would be running there.

00:36:19.130 --> 00:36:20.870
The ping time would
be much faster.

00:36:20.870 --> 00:36:22.970
You choose your GPU type.

00:36:22.970 --> 00:36:26.520
Obviously, we have
our K80s right now.

00:36:26.520 --> 00:36:27.860
And you choose your image.

00:36:27.860 --> 00:36:29.540
GPUs can attach to
an instance running

00:36:29.540 --> 00:36:32.060
any one of our public images--

00:36:32.060 --> 00:36:35.540
different flavors of Linux and
different flavors of Windows.

00:36:35.540 --> 00:36:39.215
So that's our remote
display workstation.

00:36:39.215 --> 00:36:43.400
Oh, and, finally, we want to run
an agent, which will allow us

00:36:43.400 --> 00:36:46.526
to run the Teradici protocol.

00:36:46.526 --> 00:36:48.150
Then we want to create
a render worker.

00:36:48.150 --> 00:36:50.400
But we want to create
the perfect instance

00:36:50.400 --> 00:36:52.877
that we will then replicate
in a managed instance group.

00:36:52.877 --> 00:36:54.960
So the two important points
here are, one, they're

00:36:54.960 --> 00:36:57.510
in the same zone for
maximum throughput.

00:36:57.510 --> 00:36:59.175
And, two, we don't
delete the boot disk

00:36:59.175 --> 00:37:00.300
when the machine goes away.

00:37:00.300 --> 00:37:01.550
And I'll show you why that is.

00:37:01.550 --> 00:37:03.660
So you set this
machine up perfectly,

00:37:03.660 --> 00:37:06.180
with all the software, the
hooks, and the licensing

00:37:06.180 --> 00:37:07.110
that you need.

00:37:07.110 --> 00:37:09.480
And then you take it down
and you save the disk.

00:37:09.480 --> 00:37:10.980
And you turn it
into an image, which

00:37:10.980 --> 00:37:12.510
gets turned into a template.

00:37:12.510 --> 00:37:16.200
The template gets deployed
across your managed instance

00:37:16.200 --> 00:37:17.970
groups.

00:37:17.970 --> 00:37:19.620
And the final
important point here

00:37:19.620 --> 00:37:23.460
is that you're running
a startup script when

00:37:23.460 --> 00:37:25.020
each of these machines boot.

00:37:25.020 --> 00:37:30.210
So we want to run the
V-Ray stand-alone server

00:37:30.210 --> 00:37:33.150
mode-- which, you can see here,
runs on a particular open port.

00:37:33.150 --> 00:37:38.190
And that means that we can take
from a list of these servers.

00:37:38.190 --> 00:37:42.420
These are all the machines that
3D Studio Max is rendering on.

00:37:46.450 --> 00:37:50.320
And so once the
machine boots up,

00:37:50.320 --> 00:37:55.090
you can use a command to
view the serial port output,

00:37:55.090 --> 00:37:58.060
to watch the progress
and to troubleshoot.

00:37:58.060 --> 00:38:01.090
And, finally, we launched an
entire, managed instance group,

00:38:01.090 --> 00:38:02.612
which is completely dynamic.

00:38:02.612 --> 00:38:03.820
You could size it up or down.

00:38:03.820 --> 00:38:05.278
If you want to
minimize your costs,

00:38:05.278 --> 00:38:07.570
you start with only two CPUs--

00:38:07.570 --> 00:38:09.820
or, sorry, two VMs.

00:38:09.820 --> 00:38:12.730
And then, once it's ready,
you deploy another 30 of them.

00:38:12.730 --> 00:38:16.780
And as long as the IPs are all
fed into your render options,

00:38:16.780 --> 00:38:19.480
the renderer will
start to expand and run

00:38:19.480 --> 00:38:20.510
on all these machines.

00:38:20.510 --> 00:38:22.340
It's unbelievably powerful.

00:38:22.340 --> 00:38:24.130
So when you think
that these things--

00:38:24.130 --> 00:38:28.720
these frames-- took two to four
hours on CPUs, and 18 minutes

00:38:28.720 --> 00:38:33.040
on older GPUs that were on
a different type of Nvidia

00:38:33.040 --> 00:38:36.340
cluster, we're getting almost
real-time performance here.

00:38:36.340 --> 00:38:41.360
Finally, there's
some helpful commands

00:38:41.360 --> 00:38:43.220
that I use all the time, too.

00:38:43.220 --> 00:38:47.960
For example, in the
top section here, I'm

00:38:47.960 --> 00:38:50.780
tailing the serial port output.

00:38:50.780 --> 00:38:55.640
And on the bottom, I can view
all of my running instances

00:38:55.640 --> 00:39:00.110
to get an idea of where
their IPs are and everything,

00:39:00.110 --> 00:39:02.162
and to see their
status-- whether they're

00:39:02.162 --> 00:39:03.620
being terminated
or whether they're

00:39:03.620 --> 00:39:05.030
coming up or still booting.

00:39:05.030 --> 00:39:07.950
And that is pretty
important when

00:39:07.950 --> 00:39:09.420
I want to resize
my instance groups

00:39:09.420 --> 00:39:13.710
or stop the entire
group entirely.

00:39:13.710 --> 00:39:16.700
I think we're
going to finish off

00:39:16.700 --> 00:39:19.040
by what John was
saying earlier is

00:39:19.040 --> 00:39:21.080
that the P100s, which
are display capable,

00:39:21.080 --> 00:39:22.310
are coming soon--

00:39:22.310 --> 00:39:24.470
very, very exciting.

00:39:24.470 --> 00:39:26.540
And the AMD cards, as well.

00:39:26.540 --> 00:39:28.500
I don't know the
specifics behind that.

00:39:28.500 --> 00:39:30.710
But, just to summarize
what we saw here today--

00:39:30.710 --> 00:39:32.990
we're running
V-Ray RT, by Chaos.

00:39:32.990 --> 00:39:37.650
We're running 3D Studio
Max on 128 K80 GPUs--

00:39:37.650 --> 00:39:40.640
there's 32 instances
each with 4 GPUs.

00:39:40.640 --> 00:39:42.950
And we're running over
Teradici PC over IP

00:39:42.950 --> 00:39:46.190
in a software client variant,
all running on a single Google

00:39:46.190 --> 00:39:48.420
central1 region.

00:39:48.420 --> 00:39:51.080
40 millisecond
round-trip latency,

00:39:51.080 --> 00:39:52.970
and it's 1,500 miles away.

00:39:52.970 --> 00:39:58.014
So all this is happening
in the cloud right now.

00:39:58.014 --> 00:39:59.478
Thank you.

00:39:59.478 --> 00:40:00.454
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:02.410 --> 00:40:04.300
JOHN BARRUS: Thanks, Adrian.

00:40:04.300 --> 00:40:06.055
So let's see--
what do we want you

00:40:06.055 --> 00:40:07.600
to remember, from the takeaway?

00:40:07.600 --> 00:40:09.531
First of all, thank
you for everyone

00:40:09.531 --> 00:40:10.780
that came up and gave a demo--

00:40:10.780 --> 00:40:13.810
MapD, Schlumberger,
and Adrian from Google,

00:40:13.810 --> 00:40:18.890
showing how V-Ray works with 128
K80s running at the same time.

00:40:18.890 --> 00:40:21.130
So fantastic, and fun demos.

00:40:21.130 --> 00:40:23.530
And, I guess, what I
want you to remember

00:40:23.530 --> 00:40:28.180
is if you want performance
that's scalable and--

00:40:28.180 --> 00:40:31.150
come to Google Cloud and
fire up a bunch of GPUs.

00:40:31.150 --> 00:40:33.520
And thanks for coming.

00:40:33.520 --> 00:40:36.570
[MUSIC PLAYING]

